## Management Events

Management events are asynchronous messages published by the reader when its status changes or when a configured condition occurs.

A management event is not an MQTT command response. Command responses are returned after the reader processes a command, whereas management events are published independently to the configured management-event topic.

### 1. Event types

| Event | Description | Configuration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Heartbeat](#op-heartbeat) | Periodically reports reader health, status, and configured statistics. | `managementEventConfig.heartbeat` |
| [GPI Event](#op-gpi) | Reports when a general-purpose input pin changes state. | `managementEventConfig.gpiEvents` |
| [GPO Event](#op-gpo) | Reports when a general-purpose output pin changes state. | `managementEventConfig.gpoEvents` |
| [User App Event](#op-userapp_event) | Carries a custom asynchronous message published by an installed user application. | `managementEventConfig.userappEvents` |
| [Error Event](#op-error) | Reports a configured reader error condition. | `managementEventConfig.errors` |
| [Warning Event](#op-warning) | Reports a configured reader warning condition. | `managementEventConfig.warnings` |
| [Firmware Update Progress](#op-firmwareUpdateProgress) | Reports firmware-update status and progress while an update is running. | Automatic |
| [Notification Event](#op-notification_event) | Reports supported reader status changes, including display-peripheral connections. | Automatic |

Select an event to view its configuration, schema, and examples.

### 2. Configure management event publishing

Complete the following two steps in order.

#### Step 1: Configure the destination

Use [set_importCloudConfig](set_importCloudConfig.md) to configure the broker connection and management-event publish topic.

The following example configures an MQTT destination for management events:

```json
{
  "command": "set_importCloudConfig",
  "command_id": "set-management-event-destination-1",
  "payload": {
    "endpointConfig": {
      "management": {
        "event": {
          "connections": [
            {
              "name": "MGMT_EVENTS_MQTT",
              "description": "Management event connection",
              "type": "mqtt",
              "options": {
                "enableSecurity": false,
                "endpoint": {
                  "hostName": "broker.example.com",
                  "port": 1883,
                  "protocol": "tcp"
                },
                "additional": {
                  "cleanSession": true,
                  "clientId": "reader1-management-events",
                  "debug": false,
                  "keepAlive": 60,
                  "qos": 0,
                  "reconnectDelay": 2,
                  "reconnectDelayMax": 60
                },
                "publishTopic": [
                  "factory/reader1/management-events"
                ],
                "subscribeTopic": []
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Replace `hostName`, `port`, `clientId`, and `publishTopic` with the values for your MQTT environment.

> **Note:** This example uses an unsecured TCP connection. Configure the appropriate TLS and certificate options when connecting to a secured broker.

#### Step 2: Configure event publication

Use [set_config](set_config.md) to configure heartbeat, GPI, GPO, user-application, error, and warning events.

The following example configures the heartbeat and enables selected management events:

```json
{
  "command": "set_config",
  "command_id": "set-management-events-1",
  "payload": {
    "READER-GATEWAY": {
      "managementEventConfig": {
        "heartbeat": {
          "interval": 60,
          "fields": {
            "radio_control": [
              "STATUS",
              "CPU",
              "RAM"
            ],
            "reader_gateway": [
              "CPU",
              "RAM",
              "INTERFACE_CONNECTION_STATUS"
            ],
            "system": [
              "HOSTNAME",
              "SYSTEMTIME",
              "CPU",
              "RAM"
            ]
          }
        },
        "gpiEvents": true,
        "gpoEvents": true,
        "userappEvents": true,
        "errors": {
          "antenna": true,
          "radio": true
        },
        "warnings": {
          "database": true,
          "radio_control": true
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Change the heartbeat fields and event settings according to your monitoring requirements. See the individual event pages for complete configuration options, including error and warning thresholds.

> **Note:** The `firmwareUpdateProgress` and `notification` events are published automatically to the configured management-event destination when the corresponding conditions occur. These events cannot be enabled or disabled through `managementEventConfig`.

### 3. Management event format

All management events use the same outer envelope. The value of `type` determines the structure of the event-specific `data` object.

#### Management event body

Use the **Example** tab to view sample management events. Use the **Schema** tab to review the common event envelope and event-specific `data` structures.

<!-- event-body -->

#### Event envelope fields

| Field | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `type` | string | Identifies the event and determines how to interpret `data`. |
| `timestamp` | string | Date and time when the reader generated the event, including its UTC offset. |
| `component` | string | Component that generated the event: `RG` for Reader Gateway, `RC` for Radio Control, or a user-application name. |
| `eventNum` | number | Sequence value assigned to the management event. |
| `data` | object | Event-specific information whose structure depends on `type`. |
